🟢 3. Code Obfuscation

Current

Debug build keeps symbols.

This is expected for development builds.

For production:

Strip debug symbols
Minify JavaScript/TypeScript
Optimize Rust binaries
Avoid embedding sensitive strings (API keys, secrets)

For Rust:

Cargo.toml

[profile.release]
strip = true
lto = true
opt-level = "z"
panic = "abort"
codegen-units = 1

For Tauri:

npm run tauri build

Avoid shipping debug builds.

🟢 4. Control-Flow Flattening

Current

Not configured.

This is an advanced binary-hardening technique that makes reverse engineering more difficult by restructuring program control flow.

Reality check

Rust does not provide built-in control-flow flattening.

Commercial protectors (e.g., VM-based or binary protection products) can offer this capability, but they're typically used for software IP protection and are not required for a secure application.

Instead, focus on:

Release optimization
Symbol stripping
Code signing
Runtime integrity checks
Secret management
Secure key handling

These deliver more practical security benefits for most applications.

